<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faramurz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1041–1051</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Abu Mansur Faramurz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faramurz was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</a>, founder of the Kakuyid dynasty, and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> from 1041. Pressed by the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, he surrendered Isfahan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> in 1051 and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abarkuh">Abarkuh</a> instead. His descendants governed Yazd as Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until the 12th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faramurz.jpg" alt="Faramurz" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FaramurzCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
